changing the world isn't always a good  thing even if it's with a really catchy  song a new character holds surprising  depths in the new one-piece movie and  her first song makes her plans clearer  than you think warning spoilers ahead  for one Piece Film red one Piece Film  red is the 15th movie adapted from a 25  year old manga series but its success  shows this franchise is anything but  tired film red broke records in Japanese  Cinemas netting more than 15 billion yen  in just over its first month of  availability and becoming one of the  most profitable releases to ever run in  Japanese theaters uniquely for the  series film red is something of a  musical while its characters don't  express themselves in song In  traditional musical theater fashion the  movie revolves around a new character  named utta who performs songs that  underscore the events of the movie in  total film red features seven songs  available to fans on a soundtrack EP  that effectively doubles as an album by  Adam the real life singer who performs  uda's vocals while UDA initially appears  to be an altogether happy and optimistic  pop star some heartbreaking events in  film red eventually reveal her true goal  while her purpose may be hidden from  viewers at first looking back on the  lyrics to the first song Buddha performs  in the movie hints at uda's true nature  whether  early on in one Piece Film red viewers  learned that utta became a popular  singer after broadcasting recordings of  her music all over the world in an  attempt to ease the lives of those  negatively impacted by the new age of  piracy part way into the film however  viewers find out that uda's singing  traps its listeners into a dream world  over which she has full control  furthermore in a dark twist she Harbors  intentions to end her own life taking  everyone trapped in her dream world  along with her her widely broadcast  concert and massive popularity leads to  her having influence over a significant  portion of the world's population who  she wants to help permanently escape  from suffering under piracy their decent  intentions but it's not a good plan the  first song UDA performs in film red is  titled new genesis while the song At  first seems to be about creating a  better world some lyrics betray a darker  meaning like say goodbye to everyone who  stands against us no one has the power  to stop a change that comes through  music and say goodbye to everything  that's weighing you down transcend  Beyond reality to colorful worlds of  Music the lyrics may sound innocuous  enough on the first listen but with the  benefit of hindsight uda's real plan  starts to become perfectly clear of  course uda's motivations are also  entirely understandable she sees other  people suffering and intends to use her  power to stop it when she sings lyrics  like we can choose the way of our future  try a brand new path if I change the  world she's not necessarily masking some  sort of hidden evil but simply  indicating that she wants to change the  world albeit in a misguided manner at  the film's climax Luffy naturally stops  UDA and saves the people trapped in her  dream world which isn't surprising  throughout all of one piece Luffy also  wants to change the world for the better  but does so by confronting and defeating  evil head on  UDA then serves as Luffy's foil in film  red sharing an interest and positive  change but acting on the goal in a more  antagonistic fashion film red producer  Shinji Shimizu highlighted uda's  uniqueness in an interview with anime  News Network he's said in one piece  films of the past there's always very  strong bad dare I say stereotypical  types of enemy figures that Luffy and  his gang would beat up and fight but  with this movie that is different  considering uda's appearance as a pop  Idol as opposed to a fearsome Warrior  he's certainly right about that but  regardless of her looks Buddha's  intentions to change the world  regardless of consequences are clear and  if you pay attention to the lyrics it's  all right there from the start  thank you  [Music]
 

